WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.949
Speaker 1: I want to start today with sort
of two economic stories that are kind of coming

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:08.750
together as one one, I find more interesting,
one I find less interesting.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.190
The first piece is this issue of inflation
and inflation fears, and we again have data

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:14.190
which will look at that.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.310
Prices went up again in the month of September.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:23.890
The second part of this, I really am kind
of struggling to take it totally seriously,

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:29.840
although there is there is an interesting
piece to it, which is fears of toy shortages

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.310
for the Christmas season.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:36.450
Now, the reason I have trouble sort of taking
it totally seriously is given everything that's

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:41.570
going on, everything we've had going on with
the pandemic, all sorts of different calamities

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.970
and crises and different things.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:48.360
If there's not every toy available at Christmas.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.820
It doesn't strike me as the absolute worst
thing in the world.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:58.050
But it's it's interesting insofar as it relates
to the continued supply shortages for a number

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.870
of different reasons that we've been seeing.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:04.210
So let's start with the inflation piece.

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:11.790
Reuters reporting U.S. consumer prices increased
solidly in September, poised to rise further

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:18.260
in the months ahead amid a surge in the costs
of energy products, which would cast doubt

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.300
on the Federal Reserve's view that high inflation
is transitory.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.460
This has been the debate is this are we seeing
sort of some catch up inflation relative to

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:34.010
the declines due to the 2020 pandemic due
to the pandemic in 2020, the pandemic still

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.900
going on, but the effects of the pandemic
in 2020?

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:42.850
Or are we seeing the start of some real inflation?

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:50.120
The consumer price index rose 0.4 percent
last month after climbing 0.3 percent in August,

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.280
the Labor Department said Wednesday.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.960
In the 12 months through September, the CPI
increased 5.4 percent after advancing five

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.420
point three percent year on year in August.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.830
Excluding the volatile food and energy components,
the CPI climbed 0.2 percent after edging up

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:07.830
0.1 percent.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.780
So what if you don't include food and energy?

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.430
The increase has been very modest.

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:17.770
However, food and energy are significant expenses
for many households, particularly, you know,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.660
one of the things about food and energy.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.430
And this we've talked about this before, but
I think it bears repeating there is a sort

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.310
of regressive aspect to food and energy spending.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:34.590
And what I mean by that is if you're really,
really rich.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.430
You're not necessarily going to be driving
that much more.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:43.319
You might have a car that's less energy efficient
or one that's more energy efficient.

00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:47.990
OK, if you're doing private jet travel, then
yes, if you're very rich, you actually are

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:50.360
going to spend a lot more on fuel.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.810
But in terms of like driving a car or heating
your home.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:59.680
Yes, if you have a significantly bigger home,
you'll pay a lot more to heat it.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.430
But relative to your income relative to your
net worth, it's not going to be that much

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:04.990
more so in that sense.

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:09.900
Energy cost increases, fuel and home heating,
electricity for cooling as well.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.800
They hit the poor more than they do the rich.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:13.950
Similarly, with food.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:20.930
OK, if you're wealthy, you might buy everything
organic at the most expensive grocery store.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:25.420
But just because you make 50 times more than
someone else, you're not likely to spend 50

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.680
times more on food.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:32.650
So this idea of if we exclude these two categories
energy and food, the increase isn't that much.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:37.520
Well, those are categories that disproportionately
affect the middle class, lower middle class

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.450
and the poor.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.950
Oil prices jumped Monday to the highest level
in years.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:47.560
Expensive energy products would add to accelerating
wage growth in exerting upward pressure on

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.860
inflation.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.550
The government reported last week that average
hourly earnings increased by the most in seven

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:58.190
months on a year on year basis because of
worker shortages.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:02.629
With the number of people voluntarily quitting
jobs at a record high in August, at least

00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:07.629
ten point four million unfilled positions,
wage inflation is set to rise further.

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:10.019
So this is one piece of the story.

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:17.419
The other piece, which again, I'm sort of
like, OK, I mean, I get it, but it's also.

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:24.509
It is what it is, toymakers warn delays in
stocking retail shelves could create a four

00:04:24.509 --> 00:04:26.870
month holiday hangover.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:32.809
Power outages in China, a resin shortage and
high labor costs have also strained the supply

00:04:32.809 --> 00:04:35.879
of goods and increased prices.

00:04:35.879 --> 00:04:41.509
The blockage of the Suez Canal in March is
part of the bottleneck.

00:04:41.509 --> 00:04:46.629
Some questions about shares, which we don't
care that much about, but there may be delays

00:04:46.629 --> 00:04:49.309
in getting people toys, so what?

00:04:49.309 --> 00:04:51.210
What's the big picture here?

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:57.699
We may be heading towards real inflation,
meaning not just rebound relative as an artifact

00:04:57.699 --> 00:04:59.860
relative to the pandemic.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:01.779
We said this six months ago.

00:05:01.779 --> 00:05:04.680
We need to continue watching it now.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:09.770
There's another piece that's important to
understand which is is inflation good or bad?

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:14.499
This is sort of like a fundamental question
if you accept and many of us don't.

00:05:14.499 --> 00:05:22.469
But if you if I if we accept traditional economic
thinking based on, at least for now, indefinite

00:05:22.469 --> 00:05:25.520
growth, you want a little bit of inflation.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:31.279
Why some people think, no, you don't want
prices going up, you want prices going down.

00:05:31.279 --> 00:05:32.279
That's good for people.

00:05:32.279 --> 00:05:33.610
It's good for consumers or prices.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:42.559
Stay the same people buy stuff because you
expect that prices will be a little bit higher

00:05:42.559 --> 00:05:43.830
in the future.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:44.860
Maybe not consciously.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:46.110
But let me put it a different way.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:51.370
If you're looking for a car, if you're looking
for a home, if you're looking for toys, if

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:58.669
you expect prices to go down, that would discourage
you from buying right now because if you wait,

00:05:58.669 --> 00:06:00.279
you can pay less.

00:06:00.279 --> 00:06:04.949
If everybody did that because they expected
prices to be lower in the future.

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:10.580
Nobody would buy stuff and this would actually
create create a deflationary spiral, which

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:12.069
could depress wages.

00:06:12.069 --> 00:06:15.050
It can put small businesses out of business,
et cetera.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:17.979
If you have tons of inflation, people just
can't afford stuff.

00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:18.979
Wages don't keep up.

00:06:18.979 --> 00:06:19.979
People can't afford stuff.

00:06:19.979 --> 00:06:23.550
So this is where the mentality of you want
a little bit of inflation.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.199
That's where wage growth comes from.

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:29.120
To the extent that we have wage growth, that's
where business profit comes from, etc.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:33.539
So when we talk about inflation, it's important
to understand that traditional economics doesn't

00:06:33.539 --> 00:06:36.240
say any inflation is bad.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.120
In fact, we have a sort of global economy
and status quo based on a little bit of inflation.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.050
You expect things to be a little more expensive
in the future.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:46.819
So you're going to go out and buy them today.

00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:51.490
And this creates revenues for businesses with
which they pay employees, the money with which

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:56.039
those employees go and buy things and sort
of keep this train going now if you don't

00:06:56.039 --> 00:06:58.479
believe in unlimited growth.

00:06:58.479 --> 00:07:00.969
Then at some point, the paradigm has to shift.

00:07:00.969 --> 00:07:08.289
But in terms of the immediate impact, we are
seeing sectors experience inflation that disproportionately

00:07:08.289 --> 00:07:13.849
affect the middle class, lower middle class
and the poor, and we'll just have to continue

00:07:13.849 --> 00:08:16.509
tracking those data.

